<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=musket">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Musket</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as musketeer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The musket is a muzzle-loading, smoothbore firearm that became the standard infantry weapon in many armies from the sixteenth to the nineteenth century. Its spread transformed warfare across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Portuguese musketeers helped defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wayna-daga">Wayna Daga</a> in 1543, and musket-armed infantry featured in the armies of the Safavid, Afsharid, Zand, Russian, Swedish, Ethiopian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi</a> empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/musket.jpg" alt="Musket" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matti Östling · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flintl%C3%A5sstudsare,_Flintl%C3%A5sstudsarfragment_-_Livrustkammaren_-_86495.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ethiopian-Adal War drew in both global rivals of the age: about 400 Portuguese <b>musketeers</b> fought for Ethiopia, while Ottoman musketeers fought for Adal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Nader made systematic use of <b>musket</b>-armed troops, the jazayerchis or jezzailchis, and of mobile field artillery, including swivel guns carried by camels (zamburaks), integrating firepower with his formidable cavalry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Later rulers obtained <b>muskets</b> and rifles from traders from Angola and the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ethiopia's survival in 1543 owed much to some 400 Portuguese <b>musketeers</b>, whose firepower helped Emperor Galawdewos defeat and kill Ahmad Gran at Wayna Daga.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain’s infantry, organised from the 1530s in the famous tercios of pikemen and <b>musketeers</b>, was regarded as the best in Europe for more than a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Idris Alooma in the late sixteenth century, the army underwent significant reforms, including improved organisation, the use of fortified camps, and <b>musketeers</b> trained with the help of Turkish instructors from Ottoman Tripoli.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>by 1521</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Europe</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>It may have been little more than a new name for a heavy arquebus; the word is recorded from 1499.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">Arquebus<span>1411</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">Bayonet<span>1606</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/gatling-gun">Gatling gun<span>1861</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=musket">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
